We begin by setting the coordinates of the center of the third circle as $(x,y)$. By the property of circles, the distance between the center of any two circles is equal to the sum of their radii. So, we have the following two equations:

\begin{equation}
\sqrt{(x-4)^2+(y-0)^2}=1+1,
\label{eq:circle1}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\sqrt{(x+4)^2+(y-0)^2}=1+1.
\label{eq:circle2}
\end{equation}

Next, we square both sides of Equation \eqref{eq:circle1} to eliminate the square root, giving us

\begin{equation}
(x-4)^2+(y-0)^2=4,
\label{eq:circle3}
\end{equation}

and similarly do the same for Equation \eqref{eq:circle2}, giving us

\begin{equation}
(x+4)^2+(y-0)^2=4.
\label{eq:circle4}
\end{equation}

To find the point of tangency with the third circle, we'll make use of the fact that there exists at least one point where the slopes of the tangent lines at both circle intersections are equal. Note that the point of tangency will also lie on our third circle. So let us denote the intersection points of the first two circles as $(\alpha,\beta)$ and $(\gamma, \delta)$.

Let $m_1$ and $m_2$ denote the slopes of the tangents at the intersection points. We can use the following equations describing the lines formed by the tangents:

\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
y-\beta=(m_1)(x-\alpha), \\
y-\gamma=(m_2)(x-\alpha).
\end{cases}
\label{eq:tangent}
\end{equation}

Since the slopes at the intersection points of the tangents are equal, we have

\begin{equation}
m_1=m_2.
\label{eq:equal_slope}
