\begin{equation}
	h = \sqrt{4^2 - \left(\frac{4}{2}\right)^2} = \sqrt{32 - 8} = \sqrt{24} = 4\sqrt{6}
\end{equation}

Half-height g (middle point between the two triangles' vertices) :
\begin{equation}
	g = \frac{3}{2} = \frac{3\sqrt{3}}{6}
\end{equation}

Half-base l
```scss
l = \frac{4}{2} = 2
```

By the similarity triangle property, we have a ratio of
\begin{equation}
	\frac{h}{l} = \frac{g}{h/2} \Leftrightarrow \frac{h}{l} = \frac{2g}{h} \Leftrightarrow \frac{4\sqrt{6}}{2} = \frac{6g}{\sqrt{24}} \Rightarrow g = \frac{3}{\sqrt{24}}
\end{equation}

The half-base l is the base of an equilateral triangle with the same slant height h, so their area * 2 will be the complete area of both equilateral triangles:
```
\begin{equation*}
	A_{triangle} = \frac{1}{2} \cdot \(4\sqrt{6}\) \cdot \(2\) = 8\sqrt{6}
\end{equation*}
```

To find the area of the common region, we will search for the ratio k between their high and low areas. From previous equations, we know that $k = g/l$. The area of the common region is $A_{common} = A_{triangle} \cdot k^2$. Substitute the found value:
```scss
