\boxed{\text{C}(20,2) = \frac{20!}{2!(20-2)!} = \frac{20\cdot 19}{2}}

Then, since no member can hold more than one office, the third office (treasurer) can be filled by any of the remaining members. Therefore, the number of ways to select a treasurer is:

\boxed{\text{C}(20-2,1) = \frac{(20-2)!}{1!((20-2)-1)!} = \frac{(18)!}{1!17!}}

Finally, since the order in which offices are filled does not matter, we can multiply the number of ways to choose each office.

\boxed{(\text{C}(20,2) \cdot \text{C}(18,1)) = (20\cdot 19 \cdot 18) / (2 \cdot 1) = \boxed{2550}}

