Consider the definition of an even function: $f(-x) = f(x)$.
Since both $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are even, we have:
\begin{aligned}
  f(-x) &= f(x) \\
  g(-x) &= g(x)
\end{aligned}

Now let's find $f(-x)g(-x)$, making use of the properties of even functions:
\begin{aligned}
\, \, f(-x)g(-x) &= [f(x)][-g(x)] \text{ (since even functions times each other gives another even function)} \\
  &= f(x)g(x) \text{ (since $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are even)}
\end{aligned}

However, $f(x)g(x)$ should be an even or odd function for the sum of the two functions. We can re-write $f(x)g(x)$ as:
\begin{aligned}
  f(x)g(x) &= \frac{f(x)g(x)+f(-x)g(-x)+f(-x)g(x)+f(x)g(-x)}{2} \\
  &= \frac{[f(x)g(x)+f(-x)g(-x)]+[f(-x)g(x)+f(x)g(-x)]}{2} \\
  &= \frac{f(x)[g(x)+g(-x)]+f(-x)[g(x)+g(-x)]}{2} \text{ (since both $g(x)$ and $g(-x)$ are commensurable)}
\end{aligned}

Recall the definition: $h(x)=h(-x)$ if the function $h(x)$ is even, otherwise $h(x)\neq h(-x)$ for $h(x)$ to be odd.

In this case, we have two terms: $g(x)+g(-x)$ and see that the sum could be either even or odd depending on the specific properties of the function $g(x)$.

However, if $g(x)$ is even, then $g(x)=g(-x)$ and thus $g(x)+g(-x)=2g(x)$, which is even. If $g(x)$ is odd, then $g(x)=-g(-x)$ and thus $g(x)+g(-x)=0$, which is even as well.

Therefore, $f(x)g(x)$ can be neither odd nor even because $f(x)g(x)$ is always expressible as the sum of two even functions. \boxed{neither}

