First, we need to find an orthogonal vector to the $x$-axis. This vector will be in the $y$-axis since the $x$-axis and $y$-axis are orthogonal. The $x$-axis is spanned by the vector $(1,0)$, so an orthogonal vector to this is $(0,1)$.

Next, we compute the matrix that corresponds to projection onto this vector. For a vector $\mathbf{v}$, the projection $\mathbf{proj}$ onto the direction of $\mathbf{u}$ is given by $\mathbf{proj} = \frac{\mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{v}}{\mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{u}} \mathbf{u}$. Let $\mathbf{u} = (0,1)$ and $\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{x} = (x_1, x_2)$. Then,

\begin{align*}
\mathbf{proj} &= \frac{(0,1) \cdot (x_1, x_2)}{\|(0,1)\|^2} (0,1) \\
&= \frac{x_2}{1} (0,1) \text{ (where we have used that the dot product $(0,1) \cdot (x_1, x_2) = x_2$)} \\
&= (0,x_2)
\end{align*}

Now, we can write the projection matrix as a function of the matrix of the vector, $A =
\begin{pmatrix}
x_1 & x_2 \\
0 & 0 \\
\end{pmatrix}
$. The matrix $A$ that projects onto the $y$-axis corresponds to the case when $\mathbf{v}$ has only a $y$-component, i.e., $x_1=0$ and $x_2=1$. Then the projection matrix is

\boxed{ A = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 \\
\end{pmatrix} }

