\binom{14}{8}  &= \binom{14-1}{8} + \binom{14-1}{7}
\\
\implies \binom{13}{8} &= \binom{14-1}{8} = 3003 - \binom{14-1}{7}  % plug in equation for $\binom{14}{8}$
\end{align*}

Also, since $\binom{n}{0} = \binom{n}{n} = 1$ and $\binom{n-1}{n-1} = 1$, we know that $\binom{13}{7} = \binom{13}{6} + \binom{13}{5}$. Let's find $\binom{13}{6}$ first:

