Since the volume of the cube is given in cubic meters, let's keep our calculation in meters:
```
\begin{align*}
    V &= 0.027 \text{ cubic meters } \\
    s^3 &= 0.027 \text{ cubic meters } \\
    \text{(divide by 0.027)} && s^3 &= 1 \text{ cubic meter } \\
    \text{(find cube root)} && s &= \cbrt{1 \text{ cubic meter }} = \boxed{1 \text{ meter }}
\end{align*}
```
Now, to compute the surface area, we can use the formula $SA = 6s^2$:
```
\begin{align*}
    \text{(surface area formula)} && SA &= 6s^2 \\
    \text{(substitute s)} && SA &= 6 \cdot (1 \text{ meter })^2 \\
    \text{(square the edge length)} && SA &= 6 \cdot 1^2 \\
    \text{(multiply)} && SA &= \boxed{6 \text{ square meters}}
\end{align*}
```
 Answer: 6 square meters, or 6 \text{ square meters } if you want to include the units explicitly.

